In this lesson we'll look at how to turn a recurring decimal back into a fraction. Here's the big idea: a recurring decimal is really just a fraction wearing a disguise, and we have a neat trick to reveal it. First, you let x stand for the recurring decimal. Then you multiply x by a power of ten, picking the power so that the repeating block slides neatly to the left of the decimal point. If there's a non-repeating part as well, you also multiply x by a smaller power of ten so the repeating block lines up right after the decimal point. Once everything lines up, you subtract the two equations, and the infinite repeating part disappears. What you're left with is a simple equation in x, which you solve to get your fraction. Let's try it on the easy case where the decimal is just point something repeating. If x equals that, then ten x equals that something repeating, and when you subtract, the recurring part cancels, leaving nine x equal to the single digit. So x is that digit over nine. Remember, though, to always reduce the fraction to its simplest form at the end. Now let's see it in action.